encyclopedia :akhul do
  name "Akhul"
  summary "Akhul are large canopy plants of Istrava whose water-driven gripping roots slowly move their suspended bodies between trees."
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :plant
  status :complete
  topics :ecology, :danger, :subject_planetary_life
  prevalence :rare
  appears_when all: { place: [:outer_system, :surface, :hot] }
  appears_when all: { place: [:outer_system, :garden] }
  biology "A suspended leaf crown and water reservoir supported by roots that swell, grip and shorten in sequence"
  principal_accommodation "A humid connected canopy with sound living supports and frequent water"
  descriptive_identity appearance: "A heavy green crown hangs between trunks on pale jointed roots; fresh grips show a wet red inner pad.", behavior: "Roots release and regrip over hours as the crown moves toward an opening in the canopy.", threat: "A root used as a bridge may withdraw its far grip; the moving crown can press a shelter against its support tree."
  prose <<~PROSE
    An akhul hangs from several trees at once. Its broad crown catches light above a rounded reservoir of water and leaf litter. Pale roots wrap nearby trunks, swelling their inner pads against the bark. By filling one root while draining another, the plant can draw its weight slowly toward a brighter opening. A move takes hours, and many crowns remain in a favorable position through a whole growing season.

    The older groves of #{ref :iridess} contain akhul large enough for people to shelter beneath their leaf skirts. What looks like a convenient bridge may be a root the plant is preparing to release. Climbers watch for a narrowing grip and newly wet pads elsewhere on the trunk. Binding the root can preserve a crossing briefly while bringing the crown's increasing weight onto the binding.

    Collectors at #{ref :house_tereval} buy young akhul for glazed courtyards. Their gardeners must move the surrounding supports as the crown grows. The #{ref :ilvaren, "Ilvaren"} return cramped specimens to forest keepers, sometimes finding rare #{encyclopedia_ref :ineth} nodules among the moist roots. Those nodules have made intact wild crowns attractive to a second set of buyers. Cutting down the supporting trees destroys the shelter that both plant and harvest require.
  PROSE
  cue "A pale root slowly peels away from the far trunk while a fresh red grip opens above it."
  cue "The hanging leaf skirt has moved across a familiar window since the room's occupants went to sleep."
  affordance "An established crown offers a sheltered canopy refuge and several temporary crossings between trees."
  pressure "A crossing can shift while people occupy it, and fastening one root transfers the plant's weight onto the chosen support."
  variation "Young crowns move among closely spaced supports and fit inside large planted courtyards."
  variation "Old akhul span several trees, carrying nests, water pockets and competing growth through the canopy with them."
  log "2026-09-20 — One False Step supplied attention to overlooked ecological partners; Wakeroot Elemental supplied terrain that can change its role. Applied through slow hydraulic movement and forest cultivation: https://www.rdanderson.com/stargate/episodes/episodes/02-19falsestep.htm ; https://mtg.wtf/card/m20/202/Wakeroot-Elemental."
end
